Date: October 22, 2026
System: Proxmox VE 9.x / Debian 13
Status: Installed and tested; access-control review pending
Goal
Access Voyager securely from outside my home network while keeping normal SSH authentication and local management access available.
Implementation
After fixing the APT repositories, I installed Tailscale on the Proxmox host, enabled its tailscaled service, and joined the host to my tailnet. I kept the existing ED25519 SSH authentication rather than enabling Tailscale SSH.
Verification
From my MacBook, I confirmed that Voyager responded over its Tailscale address (100.89.77.78). I tested ICMP connectivity, checked that TCP port 22 was reachable, logged in over SSH, and opened the Proxmox web interface on port 8006.

One useful connectivity test was:
nc -vz -w 5 100.89.77.78 22
An early nc attempt failed because I had omitted the port argument. Correcting the command helped separate a test-command mistake from a server problem.
Outcome
SSH and the Proxmox dashboard were accessible over Tailscale. This gave me another management path beyond the local LAN.
Remaining work
Review tailnet access rules, confirm the effective Proxmox firewall policy, and test a recovery procedure before treating remote access as fully hardened. Successful connectivity does not mean the access permissions are least-privilege.
Lessons learned
- Verify network reachability and application access independently.
- Tailscale provides a private network path; SSH still handles server authentication in this setup.
- A successful connection test confirms functionality, not necessarily security policy enforcement.
